/* book Worked example: Q: A ductile panel has σx = 100 MPa, σy = −40 MPa, τxy = 30 MPa and yield strength σY = 250 MPa. Compare Tresca, von Mises, octahedral shear and maximum principal stress; give each factor of safety.
   1. σavg = (100 − 40)/2 = 30 MPa; R = √(70² + 30²) = 76.16 MPa.
   2. σ1 = 30 + 76.16 = 106.16 MPa; σ2 = 30 − 76.16 = −46.16 MPa; σ3 = 0.
   3. Tresca: |σ1−σ2| = 152.32, |σ2−σ3| = 46.16, |σ3−σ1| = 106.16 MPa; largest 152.32 MPa. n = 250/152.32 = 1.64.
   4. von Mises: 100² − 100(−40) + (−40)² + 3(30²) = 18 300; σeq = 135.28 MPa; n = 250/135.28 = 1.85.
   5. Octahedral: sum of squares = 152.32² + 46.16² + 106.16² ≈ 36 600 MPa²; √ = 191.31 MPa; τoct = 191.31/3 = 63.77 MPa; (3/√2)(63.77) = 135.28 MPa.
   6. Maximum principal stress: n = 250/106.16 = 2.36, unsafe for a ductile metal.
   Answer: Tresca gives σeq = 152.3 MPa (n = 1.64); von Mises and octahedral shear give 135.3 MPa (n = 1.85); maximum principal stress gives n = 2.36, which is unsafe for a ductile metal. */
