/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Fatigue has two stages: crack initiation at a notch or surface defect, then crack growth under repeated stress.
   - A rotating-bending test bends a polished specimen while it spins.
   - S is stress amplitude in MPa; N is cycles to failure; A is the stress at N = 1 in MPa; b is the fatigue exponent, negative for a falling line.
   - N is plotted on a log scale because life runs from thousands to millions of cycles.
   - The endurance limit, or fatigue limit, Se, is the stress amplitude below which the material survives indefinitely.
   - Aluminium and most non-ferrous alloys have no true endurance limit.
   - Basquin's law fits the sloping part.
   - Real parts lose strength through surface finish, size, notches, corrosion and mean stress: a rough surface starts cracks earlier, a large section has more weak spots, a notch concentrates stress, corrosion pits act as cracks, and tensile me
*/
