/* book exam traps: Dividing Se by σa while a tensile mean stress acts. The mean part also spends life; use σa/Se + σm/Su. The shortcut n = Se/σa holds only at R = −1. | Writing R = σmax/σmin. It is R = σmin/σmax, and compression keeps its sign: −100/300 = −0.33, not +0.33. | Putting Su where Sy belongs. The Goodman line ends at Su, Soderberg at Sy, and the Langer line sits at Sy. | Checking the fatigue line only. A large mean stress yields the part on cycle one, so always test σa + σm against Sy. | Forgetting compressive yield. With a compressive mean stress the most compressive stress, σmin = σm − σa, can yield the part on the first cycle, so check |σmin| against the compressive yield strength too. */
